.TH OBSERVER-DASHBOARD 1 "2026-09-03" "observer 0.10.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
observer-dashboard \- watch a machine's vital signs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B observer-dashboard
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B observer-dashboard
opens a window that polls an Observer service once a second and shows what it
reports: CPU, memory, disk space per volume and disk activity per device, on
gauges, with the last hour drawn underneath. Clicking the CPU, the memory or a
disk activity gauge lists the processes consuming most of it, refreshed once a
second, and the selected one can be ended from there. The list behind a disk
gauge is ranked by I/O over the whole machine, because the counters are per
process and not per device; on Linux the service can read the I/O of other
users' processes only with CAP_SYS_PTRACE, which it deliberately lacks, so
those rows show a dash.
.PP
With no configuration it talks to the service on this machine over the local
socket, and needs no token. For that to work your account has to be in the
.B observer
group:
.PP
.RS
.B sudo usermod \-aG observer $USER
.RE
.PP
then log out and back in.
.PP
It can watch other machines too, and that is what makes watching several of
them nearly free. Each one needs two values, both printed by
.B observer share
on that machine: its address and its certificate fingerprint go into
.IR machines.json ;
its token is handed to this computer with
.B observer token set NAME
and is never written into the file. An entry that still carries an
.B apiToken
field is refused, even when the token is the right one, and is listed under the
machines with the reason.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B Observer__BaseAddress
A single service to query, for example
.IR https://other-machine:5058/ .
Defaults to the local socket. Kept for the older one-machine setup;
.I machines.json
is the normal way.
.TP
.B Observer__ApiToken
The token for that service. Not needed for this machine.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/Observer/machines.json
The machines to watch. Each entry has a
.B name
(required: it is the key the token is kept under), a
.B baseAddress
and a
.B fingerprint
of the form
.IR sha256:... .
The token does not go here.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/Observer/secrets/NAME
Where
.B observer token set
keeps each machine's token, readable only by its owner.
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/Observer/client.json
Read only when
.I machines.json
does not exist: the older one-machine configuration, with the fields
.B baseAddress
and
.BR apiToken .
It carries another machine's credential, so it is not something to share.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR observer (1)
